<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ibn Tumart</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1130">1130</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Muhammad ibn Tumart</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ibn Tumart was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> religious reformer who preached a strict doctrine of the unity of God and proclaimed himself the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a>. From the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains">Atlas Mountains</a> he led a movement against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire</a>, and died in 1130. His followers, led by Abd al-Mumin, founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a> on his teachings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ibn-tumart.jpg" alt="Ibn Tumart" width="269" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joan Serra y Pausas · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spanish_engraving_depicting_Ibn_Tumart.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its founder, <b>Muhammad ibn Tumart</b>, was a Berber theologian of the Masmuda who had studied in al-Andalus and the Islamic East and returned to the Maghreb preaching strict tawhid, the absolute unity of God, and denouncing the ruling Almoravid dynasty as anthropomorphists who ascribed physical attributes to God…</blockquote><a href="/empire/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Almohad movement, launched by <b>Ibn Tumart</b> in the Atlas Mountains around 1121, denounced the Almoravids as anthropomorphist heretics who had succumbed to luxury and oppressed their subjects with unlawful taxation, much the same charges the Almoravids had levelled against the taifas.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Hafsids took their name from Abu Hafs Umar al-Hintati, a Masmuda Berber chief from the High Atlas who was among the closest companions of <b>Ibn Tumart</b>, the founder of the Almohad movement, and a leading figure of the early Almohad Caliphate.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hafsid-sultanate">Hafsid Sultanate · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Igiliz. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tinmel">Tinmel</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=30.99,-8.23&amp;year=1120" aria-label="Tinmel on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4337 1034 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Ibn Tumart"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4787 1304h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4787 1304h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=30.99,-8.23&amp;year=1120">What was here in 1120? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
